A||||10||v8vinnie||TRANSAXLE OIL?||||1||
Z||000000||v8vinnie||02-18-2001||07:29 PM||||i just finished installing the axle adapters on the tranny,(5 speed), took some of the play out of the shafts, especially the long one on the rear right side, havent driven it yet to se if its any smother because when i poped the seal out i had to drain a little oil from the tranny, question is , are any of you guys running any thing other than regular trans oil in there, or should i use different oil?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||02-18-2001||08:39 PM||||all the stock fiero transmissions use 5-30 or 10-30 motor oil. I forget which exactly at the moment. Some people are using synthetic. others say they don't like synthetic. try a search and see what that turns up. We've covered that one allot.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||02-18-2001||09:19 PM||||I used Mobil-1's 0w-30 in my Isuzu tranny and it seemed to help make shifts a little easier and really helped at low temps. I have 5w30 in my Muncie and it seems nice and slick. However, my Honda has not had good results with the synthetic oil- it grinds going into gears now unless you granny shift it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Matt88||02-19-2001||03:54 PM||||My '88 GT had the clutch replaced just before I bought it about 3 month ago.  The repair order shows that the transaxle fluid was changed and refilled with Syncron at $7.50 per quart.<P>Shifting is stiff when cold and it acts like the syncro's aren't working right because I get a little catching when shifting from 1st to 2nd and 2nd to 3rd.  When it heats up, it shifts much smoother and cleaner.  <P>I figured this was a common problem with Fiero's because my 95 SE V6 4sp did the same thing.  I haven't tried any other fluid.  <P>GM recommends Syncron, but I'm all for trying something else if it makes the shifting cleaner and easier.||||reg||1
Z||000004||v8vinnie||02-19-2001||09:30 PM||||thanks for the replys, i think ill try a little of both maybe a semi synthetic blend?||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||02-20-2001||07:53 PM||||The fluid sold at GM dealer's parts counters seems to work the best,it's called synchromesh fluid i believe,and is around $10 a quart.||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||02-20-2001||08:21 PM||||I've always run Havoline 5W-30 in my Getrag.  Change it once a year.  I had some leftover synthetic Havoline, so I used it the last time.  Doesn't seem any different.  Never used any additives, either, but I seem to remember an older thread saying GM recommended adding some Synchromesh fluid in a TSB.  Not sure how much, though.||||reg||1
Z||000007||v8vinnie||02-20-2001||08:51 PM||||thanks again for the replys, ill think ill buy some gm fluid at a later date just for the hell of it and see if that will be a different shifting feel.||||reg||1
Z||000008||montage||02-21-2001||08:37 AM||||Go to your local quick lube place (or other Pennzoil dealer) and purchased Pennzoil synchromesh for $4.00 a quart. Same as GM lube but cheaper. My understanding is the big benefit of synchromesh is improved lubrication of non-ferrous parts. Shifts smoother and less wear.||||reg||1
Z||000009||v8vinnie||02-21-2001||06:49 PM||||well i took my car to work today and those axle adapters with the seals and bearings worked pretty good well worth the money, also i work for a toyota fork lift dealer ship and we use pennzoil products only, so ill ask for the the special oil for the gear box you recomend , talk later!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||02-22-2001||03:28 PM||||I use Dexron III ATF in both of my Getrags. They shift much smoother and more easily and don't care if its warm or cold. I've run them 8-10K miles that way without other difficulties, and without rebuilding them first. Both are high mileage units 100K+<P>My dad also uses ATF in his Saginaw 4 spd w/ Borg-Warner planetary overdrive.<BR>||||reg||1
